>> Garnier-Villarreal wrote:
>>>
>>> Jorge
>>>
>>> This happened because of the curious behavior of the pD (effective number
>>>
>>> of parameters). You are using default priors, so the bayesian effective
>>> number of parameters should be really close to the frequentist number of
>>>
>>> parameters (npar). In the simulation testing the fit indices, the LOO and
>>>
>>> WAIC based pD performed better and closer to the expected behavior.
>>>
>>> For reasons I cannot understand without the data set, you LOO and WAIC
>>> pDs are higher (~ 20) than the frequentist 15. So this is causing a
>>> problem
>>> with the calculations as turns the "degrees of freedom" into negative.
>>> Weirdly enough the DIC pD is close to the expected npar.
>>>
>>> So,
>>> - First: check that your model has converged. Meaning the PSRF is lower
>>> than 1.05 for every parameter blavInspect(fit1, "psrf"). If not, run the
>>>
>>> model for longer burnin, and check the pDs
>>> - Second: If this bechavior of the pDs is there whit a converged model,
>>> change to use the DIC based pD for the fit indices calculations ML <-
>>> blavFitIndices(fit1, baseline.model = fit0, pD="dic")
>>>
>>>
>>> Hope this helps
